{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And it shall be when your children say to you, 'What is this service to you?'</b> In the Passover Haggadah it concludes that in this verse it speaks of the wicked son, who speaks mockingly — 'What is this service?' — from the expression 'מָה אֱנוֹשׁ' (Psalms 8:5), and it concludes there: 'You too should blunt his teeth and say to him, \"בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי בְּצֵאתִי מִמִּצְרָיִם\" (Exodus 13:8) — to me and not to him; had he been there, he would not have been redeemed.' And one presses the question on this: why did it not cite the answer given right beside it in the Torah, where it says (Exodus 12:27) 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳' and so on? Instead it took for itself an answer from a verse that is not adjacent to it in the Torah. And furthermore, this verse had already been expounded regarding the one who does not know how to ask — yet for him the Haggadah did not make the precise inference 'to me and not to him.' So how can a single verse be expounded to yield two contradictory answers? And Maharai raised these difficulties in his book 'זֶבַח פֶּסַח' and entered into great stretches and forced solutions that do not satisfy the heart. And now open your eyes and see, for behold, I shall explain the entire passage of the four sons in which the Torah spoke, in such a way that all the doubts and questions that Maharai raised will be resolved, and I have seen fit to resolve the doubts mentioned regarding the answer to the wicked son in three different ways." "The first way is this: the verse 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח' and so on is not connected at all with the verse above it; rather it is an independent statement. And the proof for this is that it should have said 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם אֲלֵיהֶם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא,' in the manner that it mentioned in the answers to all the sons — 'וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ,' 'וְאָמַרְתָּ אֵלָיו.' Why then did it change here and write 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח' without specifying to whom it is said? And there is a further difficulty: why did it say in every place regarding the Paschal offering 'וְעָבַדְתָּ אֶת הָעֲבֹדָה' (Exodus 13:5), yet here it said 'וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת הָעֲבֹדָה'? And there is a further difficulty: in every place it referred to sons in the singular — for there it says (Exodus 13:8) 'וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ,' and regarding the simple son it says (Exodus 13:14) 'וְהָיָה כִּי יִשְׁאָלְךָ בִנְךָ לֵאמֹר מַה זֹּאת,' and regarding the wise son it says in parashat Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 6:20) 'כִּי יִשְׁאָלְךָ בִנְךָ מָחָר לֵאמֹר מָה.' But here it says 'וְהָיָה כִּי יֹאמְרוּ אֲלֵיכֶם בְּנֵיכֶם' — in the plural." "But surely here it speaks of a time when many rebellious sons will arise and plot to destroy this cornerstone of worship and to make the people cease from the labor of the worship of HaShem, blessed be He; and on account of those who seek to destroy, the worship requires extra vigilance. And concerning that rebellious generation it said 'וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת' (Exodus 12:17) — you shall guard it so that it not be destroyed. And afterward it explained at what time the worship requires guarding; regarding this it said 'וְהָיָה כִּי יֹאמְרוּ אֲלֵיכֶם בְּנֵיכֶם מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם' (Exodus 12:26) — that is, this guarding will be needed at that time when your children — destructive sons — will arise and say mockingly 'מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם,' for 'בְּלַעֲגֵי שָׂפָה יְדַבְּרוּ אֶל הָעָם הַזֶּה' (Isaiah 28:11), in order to make them cease from the labor of the worship of HaShem. And concerning that time it said 'וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת הָעֲבֹדָה,' for then it requires guarding all the more, to stand against the mockers. And therefore it used 'בְּנֵיכֶם' in the plural, for on account of any single mocker it would not require guarding so much, for his opinion is nullified before everyone. And according to this, the first verse is not connected with the verse that follows it." "And what is said afterward, 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳' (Exodus 12:27), is an independent statement — because Rabban Gamliel said: whoever did not say these three things at Passover has not fulfilled his obligation, and they are: Pesach, Matzah, and Maror. And from where does Rabban Gamliel derive this, that in addition to performing the commandments in practice one is obligated to say and recount with the mouth their significance? Surely he learned this from the verse 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא' and so on, which is a commandment regarding verbal declaration in addition to the doing. And still it had not yet been mentioned that there is an obligation to recount with the mouth the matter of matzah and maror, for in this verse only 'זֶבַח פֶּסַח' is mentioned. Therefore it is said afterward, 'וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא לֵאמֹר בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי בְּצֵאתִי מִמִּצְרָיִם' (Exodus 13:8), and the master of the Haggadah expounded: 'בַּעֲבוּר זֶה' — I said this only at a time when matzah and maror are placed before you. And one presses the question on this: why did it not also mention the pesach and say 'at a time when pesach, matzah, and maror are placed before you'? Rather, surely because it had already mentioned the obligation of verbal declaration regarding the matter of the pesach in the verse 'וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא'; but regarding the matter of matzah and maror it had not yet mentioned that one is obligated to recount them with the mouth even if no one asks about them. If so, the verse 'וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ' speaks of matzah and maror specifically, that one is obligated to recount them with the mouth before the members of his household even if they do not ask him at all." "And according to this, no answer has yet been mentioned in the Torah for the wicked son, who says mockingly 'מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם.' And the view of the master of the Haggadah is that the verse 'וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ' is the answer to the wicked son and to the son who does not know how to ask. For one presses the question on the words of the master of the Haggadah, who expounded this verse regarding the one who does not know how to ask: from where does he derive that the son is silent because he does not know how to ask? Perhaps he is silent because he is thoroughly wicked and does not even want to speak of this commandment, for it is so contemptible in his eyes. And thus Maharai also raised this difficulty, but he did not resolve anything — for the answer stated in the Torah is no proof at all regarding the intent of the son, for who knows his intent, whether for good or for ill (Sanhedrin 26a)? And it is not in the Torah's hand to give us such prophecy, to state before HaShem, blessed be He, a revelation of his intent — for before Him, blessed be He, it is revealed; but as for us, we cannot know, and before whom is it revealed? And is it so definitive a matter that every son who is silent — that must be because he does not know how to ask?" "Therefore my heart tells me that this is not the way; rather, the explanation of the matter is thus: For this inference 'to me and not to him' is not understood from the language of 'to me' on its own, and the inference depends on the disposition of the son sitting before him. For if this son who is silent is wicked and the worship counts for nothing in his eyes, then the inference 'to me and not to him' is well taken, because he says to him that it was בַּעֲבוּר עֲבוֹדָה זוֹ — on account of this worship — that HaShem did for me, when I went out of Egypt, all the great deeds and all the awesome wonders, and it follows from this that had I not engaged in this worship, I would still not have been redeemed. And the same is true of this wicked son: had he been there, he certainly would not have wanted to perform this worship, and then he necessarily would not have been redeemed. But if this son who is silent does not know how to ask, and the worship is not contemptible in his eyes, if so, who is to say that had he been there he would not have performed this worship? Most certainly, had he been there he too would have performed that worship, and without doubt he too would have been redeemed. And the phrase 'to me and not to him' is not inferred with regard to him, for the expression 'to me' is not an inference at all on its own, since the father was required to say to the son the expression 'to me,' for the son was not in that generation, so how could he say 'HaShem did for us'? Rather, the inference comes from the disposition of the son — whether he believes in this worship, that it has substance, or not. For if he does not believe in it, then certainly he would not have engaged in it had he been there, and then he necessarily would not have been redeemed — for it was on account of this worship that HaShem did for me, when I went out of Egypt, the Exodus and the rest of the great deeds, and whoever would not have engaged in it would not have been redeemed. But if the son believes in the worship, except that he lacks the understanding to ask, then certainly had he been there he too would have engaged in this worship and he too would have been redeemed. And the Torah said: if it happens that your son sits before you and puts his hand to his mouth and asks you nothing, and the matter is in doubt for you — whether he is silent because he does not know how to ask, or because he is an evil wicked person — behold, in order to escape from this perplexity, go out and say to him, ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי בְּצֵאתִי מִמִּצְרָיִם״ — and then, whichever way it turns out, you fulfill your obligation. For if he is wicked at heart, then you have blunted his teeth by saying ״עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״, for since he does not acknowledge the worship, the meaning ״לִי וְלֹא לוֹ״ — 'to me and not to him' — is understood. And if he is not wicked but merely does not know how to ask, then say this verse to him and from it ״לִי וְלֹא לוֹ״ will not be inferred, for after all, had he been there he certainly would also have engaged in this worship." "<b>And for this reason it changed here to say 'and you shall tell your son.'</b> And it does not say 'and you shall say' (וְאָמַרְתָּ) or 'and you shall speak' (וְדִבַּרְתָּ), but rather because the term הַגָּדָה serves one meaning and its opposite: it serves as a term for hard things like sinews (גִידִין), as Rashi explained on the verse ״וְתַגֵּיד לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 19:3) — and even though there he explained it that way because of the letter yod, nevertheless even without a yod its meaning is hard things like sinews; and it also serves as a term for softness, as our Sages of blessed memory said in the chapter Ein Dorshin (Hagigah 13a): 'Aggadah that draws a person's heart like water,' and so it is inferred in the chapter Rabbi Akiva (Shabbat 88b), for there they interpreted the verse ״וַיַּגֵּד מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 19:9) which bears two faces. Therefore the expression ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ״ is stated here: if he does not know how to ask, then draw his heart to the worship of HaShem, blessed be He, as it is written ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ לָכֶם צֹאן״ (Exodus 12:21) — that is to say: draw the hearts of your children to the worship of HaShem. And likewise 'aggadah' is a term of drawing, as stated, by means of soft words. And if he is wicked at heart, then with these words you feed him hard things like sinews that blunt a person's teeth, because he will understand from your words ״לִי וְלֹא לוֹ״ — 'to me and not to him.' And from here the author of the Haggadah learned to say 'and you too blunt his teeth,' etc., and he learned the reply to the wicked son from the verse ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ״ — for if the Torah said to speak thus to the son who is silent out of doubt, lest he be wicked, how much more so should you reply thus to the wicked son who says with a full mouth, by way of mockery, ״מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם״. And this interpretation is precious. And behold, there is room for me to explain this further in different ways, and the discerning reader will choose from among them what seems good in his eyes." "The second way is to accommodate the view of most Torah commentators who hold that the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא״ is linked with the preceding verse, and it is a reply to the wicked son and to the rebellious ones who sought to destroy the cornerstone of the worship in the manner explained above. And we may say that the intent of the verse is to say that you should answer the wicked son his own reply that stands beside him, namely the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳״ — for everything understood from the verse ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי בְּצֵאתִי מִמִּצְרָיִם״ is also understood from this verse, for the wicked son says ״מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם״ — 'to you' and not to him, for he thinks there is no substance to this worship and it is nothing but for your own enjoyment, since you are the ones eating and drinking. And you shall say to him: ״זֶבַח פֶּסַח הִיא לַה׳״ — it is a sacrifice to HaShem and not for us. And the proof for this — that He passed over the houses of the children of Israel when He struck Egypt, and He saved our houses. And how can you say there is no substance to it? Behold, everyone who engaged in this worship was saved from the plague, and the Egyptians who did not engage in this worship were struck, and He saved our houses on account of this worship. And from this verse the inference ״לִי וְלֹא לוֹ״ is drawn, for the wicked son admits that had he been there he would not have performed this worship, and if so he certainly would not have been redeemed, but rather would have been struck together with the Egyptians who did not engage in this worship and were struck. And what is written, 'and you shall say to him ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״' — the meaning is not that you should say to him that verse which is stated regarding one who does not know how to ask, but rather the intent is to answer him in the form of the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳״. And the reason the verse is not quoted verbatim is because it goes on to say ״וְאַף אַתָּה הַקְהֵה אֶת שִׁנָּיו״, and he had not yet explained what is the thing that blunts his teeth; therefore he gave the explanation on the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא״, and said that from this verse it is understood that it was on account of this Paschal sacrifice that HaShem did for me when I went out from Egypt, and saved us from the plague. And from this the wicked son will automatically understand ״לִי וְלֹא לוֹ״ — for had he been there, he certainly would not have performed the worship, and then he would not have been redeemed but would have been struck with the Egyptians — and thereby you will blunt his teeth." "<b>And what is written with the expression 'af' — to say 'and you too blunt his teeth.'</b> For our Sages of blessed memory said (Seder Olam) that when Israel tied the lamb to the legs of the bed, the teeth of the Egyptians were blunted, for the Egyptians too mocked this commandment, for they thought everything depended on the zodiac of the lamb (טָלֶה), and this is precisely the view of this wicked son. And immediately, when this wicked son sees Israel engaged in this commandment, his teeth are blunted of themselves, as it is written (Psalms 112:10): ״רָשָׁע יִרְאֶה וְכָעָס, שִׁנָּיו יַחֲרֹק וְנָמָס״ — 'The wicked man shall see and be angry; he shall gnash his teeth and melt away.' And in addition to the blunting of his teeth that were already blunted of themselves, you too shall add to blunt his teeth, and say to him the explanation of the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳״ (Exodus 12:27), from which it is understood ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״ and not to him, etc. And had he brought him the verse verbatim, perhaps the wicked son would not have understood from it this explanation that blunts his teeth. But the verse stated nearby — ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא לֵאמֹר בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״ (Exodus 13:8) — even though there 'to me' (לִי) is stated explicitly, nevertheless one cannot infer from it 'to me and not to him,' because the expression 'to me' is not an inference at all, for how could the father say to the son 'HaShem did for us,' when the son was not there at the time of the Exodus from Egypt? Therefore the father was required to say 'to me.' And also one cannot infer 'to me and not to him,' for had he been there, would he not have performed this worship? For who is to tell us that he would not have performed the worship? Most certainly he too would have performed it, and he would have been redeemed with all his brothers the children of Israel. But in the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳״, it is well understood that the meaning of the verse is 'to me and not to him,' for there it explicitly explains that those engaged in the worship were saved from the plague, and the Egyptians who did not engage in it were struck. And if so, this wicked son who speaks with mockery of speech, ״מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם״ — 'to you' and not to him — and who has removed himself from the collective, he certainly, had he been there, would not have performed this worship, and then he necessarily would not have been redeemed but would have been struck with the Egyptians. And this inference is correct and precious." "The third way is to accommodate the view of the commentators of the Torah and the Aggadah who hold that the view of the author of the Haggadah is to answer the wicked son with the verse ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״ (Exodus 13:8), which is expounded regarding one who does not know how to ask. And the view of the author of the Haggadah is that the Torah wrote two replies to the wicked son: one reply is that stated beside him in the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳״ (Exodus 12:27). The second reply is alluded to in the verse ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא לֵאמֹר בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״ etc. (Exodus 13:8). And his view is that in the verse ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ״ there is a reply to the wicked son and to one who does not know how to ask, and his proof is from the fact that it does not say ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״ — for אֲמִירָה is soft and every דִּבּוּר is hard, and the expression הַגָּדָה is intermediate and encompasses both: a term for hard things like sinews (גִידִין) that blunt a person's teeth, and for soft things that draw a person's heart, as explained in the first way above. And this is a hint that the verse wanted the father to say this verse to two sons — both to this son who does not know how to ask, to tell and draw his heart to the worship of HaShem, blessed be He, as it is written ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ״ etc. (Exodus 12:21), and to say to him ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״ etc.; and we already said above that for this son who does not know how to ask there is no inference at all from the expression ״לִי״ to say 'to me and not to him.' But to the rebellious son you shall tell hard things like sinews, and by feeding him hard things like sinews you shall blunt his teeth, and you shall also say to him this verse, ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״, and 'to me and not to him' is well inferred with regard to him, for had he been there he would not have performed this worship, and then he certainly would not have been redeemed — for even to me HaShem did nothing except on account of this. And had this verse been written for the son who does not know how to ask alone, it should have said ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״ — soft speech. And had it been written only for the wicked son alone, it should have said ״וְדִבַּרְתָּ״ — hard speech. And furthermore, this verse was not attached to the mockery and contempt of the wicked son's words, but it wrote ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ״, which includes both meanings, as stated." "<b>And it shall come to pass, when your children say to you, 'What do you mean by this service?'</b> The way of the Torah is to test the wicked person first — is it possible to draw him to the service of HaShem, blessed be He, with gentle words? And this is the answer that stands beside the question: ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא לַה׳״ (Exodus 12:27), and the word ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם״ — 'and you shall say' — is a gentle saying, for in this verse there is no implication of 'to me' or 'to him,' since the expression ״לִי״ is not written explicitly in this verse. But if he stiffens his neck and does not heed the voice of whisperers, then ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ״ (Exodus 13:8) — to feed him harsh words, like sinews, to blunt his teeth with them, and to say to him ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה עָשָׂה ה׳ לִי״ — 'to me' and not 'to him.' And this is what is said: ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ״ (Exodus 12:21) — ״מִשְׁכוּ״ is a term of drawing, that you should draw Israel at the beginning with gentle words to the service of the paschal offering, and if they do not heed, then ״וּקְחוּ״ — a taking by hand, forcibly; and harsh words are comparable to physical compulsion by hand, which is precisely a taking." "And as for why the author of the Haggadah did not mention the answer that is stated beside the question — ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם זֶבַח פֶּסַח הוּא״ — it may be that he alluded to it in what he said: 'And you too — blunt his teeth.' What is the force of the word 'too'? Rather, he meant to say: this, I do not need to tell you — that you should give him the answer that stands beside the question, for it is obvious that you will certainly give him that answer. Rather, 'too' means: also the second answer, which speaks of blunting the teeth of the wicked — that answer too you shall give him if he is not chastened by the first answer. If so, he alluded in the word 'too' also to the answer that stands beside it, in the manner of 'not only this, but also this.' For this is why the Torah wrote two answers for the wicked son: one gentle and one harsh. And the author of the Haggadah said: do not cower before the wicked one; and in addition to the first gentle answer, you shall give him also this second answer, to blunt his teeth with it. And do not marvel at the desire that one verse should be expounded in two contradictory ways, for we have already said that the inference does not derive from the word ״לִי,״ and the essence of the inference depends on 'between one who serves Elohim and one who has not served Him' (Malachi 3:18). Therefore, for a wicked person who did not serve Him, the inference is precise: 'to me and not to him.' But the son who does not know how to ask — had he been there, he would have served Him; therefore the inference 'to me and not to him' does not apply to him, and had he been there, he too would have been redeemed."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "Maharai refers to R. Isaac Aboab; the work cited is his Passover commentary. The phrase מָה אֱנוֹשׁ is read as an expression of contempt per the Haggadah's midrash."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:03.708581403Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}